== Introduction
<introduction-9>
This class covers selected aḥādīth from #strong[Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ],
Kitāb al-Nikāḥ (Book of Marriage). The discussion includes the
qualities of a righteous wife, the spiritual significance of marriage in
Islam, the principle of simplicity in nikāḥ, and the permissibility of
looking at a prospective spouse before proposing.

#horizontalrule

== Ḥadīth 1: Four Qualities of a Good Wife
<four-qualities-good-wife>

A good wife is summarized in four qualities:

+ #strong[Muṭīʿah] (obedient) --- she is obedient to her husband in
  matters that do not contradict the Sharīʿah.
+ #strong[Pleasing to look at] --- when her husband looks at her, she is
  pleasing to him. This encompasses both outward beauty (physical
  appearance) and inner beauty (beauty of character).
+ #strong[Obeys his instructions] --- when her husband directs her
  regarding household or other permissible matters, she complies.
+ #strong[Protects her chastity in his absence] --- she guards herself,
  her honor, and his household when he is not present.

=== The Path to Jannah for a Woman
<path-jannah-woman>
If a woman fulfills her obligations towards Allah (prays five times,
etc.) and lives such a life that her husband is pleased with her, then
according to the ḥadīth, #strong[all eight doors of Jannah are opened
for her], and she may enter through whichever door she wishes.

#strong[Shaykh's commentary:] For a woman, entry into Jannah requires
two things --- pleasing Allah and pleasing her husband. For a man, the
path involves additional responsibilities: earning a livelihood, daʿwah,
amr bil maʿrūf wa nahy ʿan al-munkar, jihād, and striving outside the
home.

=== Note on Physical Beauty
<note-physical-beauty>
Islam does not sideline the dimension of physical beauty. It is
important for a healthy marriage. A woman may attend to her appearance
#strong[with the right intention] (for the sake of her husband), and it
becomes rewarding for her.

#horizontalrule

== Ḥadīth 2: Marriage is Half of Dīn
<marriage-half-deen>

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["When a servant marries, he has completed half of his religion.
Now let him fear Allah in the remaining half."]
]

=== Why is Nikāḥ "Half of Īmān"?
<why-half-iman>
A person has two categories of obligations:

+ #strong[Ḥuqūq Allāh] (rights of Allah) --- worship, ṣalāh, ṣawm,
  etc.
+ #strong[Ḥuqūq al-ʿIbād] (rights of people) --- responsibilities
  towards others.

The relationship of nikāḥ is the most delicate and sensitive of all
human relationships. The Prophet ﷺ said:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["The best of you are those who are best towards their wives."]
]

If a person fulfills the rights of the marital relationship properly, it
is expected that their other relationships will also be healthy. Thus,
nikāḥ effectively represents the #strong[ḥuqūq al-ʿibād] half of dīn.

#strong[Before marriage:] No matter how righteous a person is, they are
fulfilling at best 50% of their dīn.

#strong[After marriage:] The other half becomes available. Even the
reward of ṣalāh increases --- it is mentioned that ṣalāh is multiplied
by #strong[27 times] once a person is married.

#horizontalrule

=== Comparison with Other Religious Traditions
<comparison-religions>

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (auto, auto, auto),
    table.header([Tradition], [Women's Spirituality], [View on Marriage]),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Islam]],
    [Complete parity --- a woman can be a waliyyah of Allah. Equal
    access to Jannat al-Firdaws.],
    [Marriage is #strong[required] to complete Īmān. A spiritual
    asset, not a hindrance.],
    [#strong[Catholicism]],
    [Only men can attain highest spiritual ranks (pope, bishop,
    cardinal).],
    [Celibacy required for highest spirituality. Women seen as
    obstacles.],
    [#strong[Hinduism]],
    [Only men can attain highest spiritual rank (yogi, Brahman).],
    [Marriage is a hindrance to spiritual development.],
    [#strong[Buddhism]],
    [Nirvāṇa reserved for men. Women cannot attain it.],
    [Even accidental touch of a woman "defiles" a man spiritually.],
  )]
  , kind: table
)

#strong[Key point:] Islam gives complete religious equality to men and
women in both #strong[dunyawī spirituality] (closeness to Allah) and
#strong[ukhrawī spirituality] (access to Jannat al-Firdaws). And unlike
other traditions, marriage is not a hindrance but a #strong[requirement]
for completing one's faith.

#horizontalrule

== Ḥadīth 3: The Greatest Barakah in Nikāḥ is Simplicity
<greatest-barakah-simplicity>

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["The greatest of nikāḥ in terms of blessings is that nikāḥ where
the burden is the least."]
]

=== Teaching: Keep Nikāḥ Simple
<keep-nikah-simple>
- The #strong[mehr] should be such that the man can easily give it.
- The #strong[functions] should not be a burden for either family.
- If Allah has given wealth and spending generously is easy and not
  burdensome, that is fine --- the teaching is contextual.
- #strong[Warning:] When nikāḥ becomes burdensome, the barakah is lost,
  and the effects show in the marital life itself.

=== Q&A: Should We Invite Everyone?
<invite-everyone>
#strong[Q:] If we invite more guests, will there be more barakah?

#strong[A (Teacher):] The Ṣaḥābah were so simple that they would
sometimes get married and the Prophet ﷺ would only find out later. He
would then make duʿāʾ for them. There was no takaluf (formality).
Whoever was available was enough. It can be a small family affair. The
more ease and simplicity, the more barakah.

=== Q&A: Children's Desires for Grand Weddings
<grand-wedding-desires>
#strong[Q:] Children see grand weddings among peers and want the same.
How do we address this?

#strong[A (Teacher):] One must gently explain the teachings of the Sunnah
--- that simplicity brings the greatest barakah. This requires long-term
tarbiyah: raising children with love for the Prophet ﷺ and his
teachings, so they are willing to sacrifice customs and trends. The model
is the Prophet ﷺ, not the culture around us.

#horizontalrule

== Ḥadīth 4: Looking at the Prospective Spouse
<looking-prospective-spouse>

=== Ruling
<ruling-looking>
According to the Ḥanafī school and the majority of ʿulamāʾ, it is
#strong[permissible] --- in fact, #strong[mustaḥabb (recommended)] ---
for a man to look at the woman he intends to propose to.

=== The Ḥadīth
<hadith-looking>
#quote(block: true)[
A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he intended to marry an Anṣārī
woman. The Prophet ﷺ said: #emph["Go and look at her, for there is
something in the eyes of some of the Anṣār."]
]

The Prophet ﷺ recommended looking because there may be a physical
characteristic that could later become a source of discomfort.

=== Practical Process
<practical-process>
The looking should not be the first step. The recommended sequence:

+ #strong[Initial inquiry:] Send a female relative (mother, sister) to
  gather information --- compatibility, character, background.
+ #strong[Assessment:] If everything looks good,
+ #strong[Physical meeting:] The man can then meet and look at the
  prospective bride.

=== What May Be Seen
<what-may-be-seen>
- The #strong[face] and #strong[hands] only.
- She does not need to wear niqāb during this meeting.
- Other parts must remain concealed.
- She may also look at him.

=== Additional Points
<additional-points-looking>
- #strong[Photographs:] Sharing pictures beforehand is also permissible.
- #strong[Even if decided:] The teacher's shaykh advised that even if
  you have already decided, still meet in person --- to practice the
  sunnah and earn the reward.
- #strong[Right to say no:] It is better to decline upfront than to
  proceed and have an unhappy marriage.

#horizontalrule

== Ḥadīth 5: A Woman Must Not Describe Another Woman to Her Husband
<woman-not-describe>

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["A woman must not touch the body of another woman \[without garment
in between\], and she must not describe another woman to her husband,
such that it is as if he were looking at her."]
]

A woman should be careful #strong[not to describe another woman's
physical appearance] to her husband, because:
- It could put waswasah (sinful thoughts) in the husband's mind.
- She would be held responsible and sinful for doing so.

#horizontalrule

